  "notes": "A multi-instrumentalist producer of mid-2000s teen and piano-pop: 76–120 straight with the backbeat on 2&4, bright majors (A major at about 90 on the 2004 single) and minor four-chord loops, \"with the same loop under every section and **the lift coming from density**\" — which is why one family dominates and the arrangement carries the dynamic rather than the harmony. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"8th hat with a 16th push into the backbeat\" (an 8th base with the fill density lifted so the push lands), and \"handclaps only in the chorus\"; two more constraints close the block — \"fills no longer than one beat\" (a 16-bar schedule with near-zero hat rolls) and \"**the drums often enter at the first chorus rather than bar 1**\", which is `dropOutBeats` at 0.9. The bass rule is stated with a floor and is encoded literally: roots with an octave jump at the chorus and **nothing below E1**, \"keeping the low end tidy under piano\" — the register floor sits at MIDI 28. The palette splits by record — acoustic strum with a double-time electric counter-strum on the teen-pop side, piano ostinato with glockenspiel and viola/cello lines on the piano-pop side — which is the triangle lane and the arp-plus-answer counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so and marks the drum specifics **[LOW-CONF]**; per-instrument attribution is partly inferred. ⚠ Every drum probability here is **INFERRED**, as is `bpm.mode` 90.",
